<?xml version='1.0' encoding='UTF-8'?>
<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>5.7.1: Patterns in Real Numbers</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01366</md:content-id>
    <md:title>5.7.1: Patterns in Real Numbers</md:title>
    <md:uuid>ae2b4d51-ef57-4c57-bc4e-795614463d3b</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para>In the table, find as many patterns as you can. Use one or more patterns to help you complete the table. Be prepared to explain your reasoning.</para>

<table class="os-raise-horizontaltable">
  <tgroup cols="6"><tbody><row>
      <entry><emphasis> fraction </emphasis></entry><entry> \(\frac12\) </entry>
      <entry> \(\frac14\) </entry>
      <entry> \(\frac18\) </entry>
      <entry> \(\frac{1}{16}\) </entry>
      <entry> \(\frac{1}{32}\) </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry><emphasis>decimal</emphasis></entry><entry> 0.5 </entry>
      <entry> 0.25 </entry>
      <entry> 0.125 </entry>
      <entry> a._____ </entry>
      <entry> b._____ </entry>
    </row>
  </tbody></tgroup></table>
<!--Interaction End -->
<section class="check-understanding"><title>Warm Up Activity</title><exercise id="cb38133c-fc76-4a75-bb30-617cc34e630d" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>What is the value for a?</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! 0.0625</para><para>The correct answer is: 0.0625</para></solution></exercise><exercise id="_5873a187-8e12-472a-bba2-e3dc88a7f04d" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>What is the value for b?</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! 0.03125</para><para>The correct answer is: 0.03125</para></solution></exercise></section><!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Describe the pattern you used to find the values that completed the table.</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    
  
  <para>Compare your answers:</para>
    
    <list>
      <item> Each number is half of the previous number. We can find the next number by multiplying the current number by \(\frac12\) or by 0.5, or by dividing by 2.</item>
      <item> The non-zero digits of the decimals are powers of 5 (\(5^1\), \(5^2\), \(5^3\), etc.), and each time there is an additional decimal place.</item>
      <item> 0.5 can be written as \(\frac{5}{10}\), 0.25 as \(\frac{25}{100}\), and 0.125 as \(\frac{125}{1,000}\). The numerators are powers of 5, and the denominators are powers of 10, so the next two numbers are \(\frac{5^4}{10^4}\), which is \(\frac{65}{10,000}\) or 0.0625, and \(\frac{5^5}{10^5}\), which is \(\frac{3,125}{100,000}\) or 0.03125.</item>
      <item> The denominators in the fractions are powers of 2.</item>
    </list>
  
  </solution></exercise></section>
</content>
